Hi Ihnwhi,

Thanks for the update about warmup and prior sampling. Also

On Mon, 2020-12-07 at 23:54 -0800, Ihnwhi Heo wrote:
> From the blavaan object, the log marginal likelihood (Laplace approximation) is -3153.237. On the other hand, the log marginal likelihood from the bridge sampling is 0.014. When I try the same after setting the prisamp argument to FALSE (i.e., posterior samples), the estimates are close to each other. Can has_cov = 0 and has_data = 0 in the semstan.rda have tremendous effects on this differing log marginal likelihood estimate? If there are other possibilities, I would like to know the origin of this difference.

This is a place where the prisamp argument is new, and the blavaan output is not yet labeled informatively. I think that Stan is giving you only the prior log-likelihood, without the data being involved at all. In contrast, blavaan is giving you the likelihood evaluated at the draws from the prior distribution (where the likelihood involves the observed data). I'm not sure what is most useful to you...

> Also, can I only extract the log marginal likelihood from the Laplace approximation in blavaan with the blavInspect() function? I tried to find it but could not.

I think you want something like

fitMeasures(fit, 'margloglik')

Ed

